    {
      "title": "The Cost of Trying to Fix a Failed 1031 Exchange",
      "source": "REJournals",
      "link": "https://rejournals.com/the-cost-of-trying-to-fix-a-failed-1031-exchange/?utm_source=rss&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=the-cost-of-trying-to-fix-a-failed-1031-exchange",
      "published": "Mon, 10 Aug 2026 10:00:00 +0000",
      "page_url": "https://altmanr91.github.io/CRE-News-Reader/articles/2026-08-10-006.html",
      "transaction_type": null,
      "article_type": "CRE Legal / Regulatory",
      "market": null,
      "narrative": "The article analyzes the landmark tax case Dobrich v. Commissioner, which serves as a cautionary tale for real estate investors attempting Section 1031 exchanges. The case highlights the strict, unforgiving nature of the 45-day identification deadline for replacement properties. The taxpayers in Dobrich not only failed to meet this deadline but also attempted to falsify and backdate documents to create a fraudulent paper trail, resulting in a 75% civil fraud penalty in addition to the loss of tax deferral. The key takeaway for investors is the critical importance of meticulous pre-sale planning and strict adherence to all 1031 exchange timelines, emphasizing that honesty and integrity are paramount even when an exchange appears to have failed.",
      "data_points": null,
